{"parentUuid":"f5bb5798-4bec-4125-85eb-3d0803f00ff9","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"0888e9cf-982e-4295-bf22-57ee9e34505b","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"dapper-whistling-anchor","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WxLziHatAaPWFYKSmHujEX","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→\n     2→<div class=\"problem-box\">\n     3→  <div class=\"problem-text\" id=\"solved\">\n     4→    <div id=\"prize\"><span class=\"tooltip\">\n     5→  DISPROVED (LEAN)\n     6→  <span class=\"tooltiptext\">\n     7→    This has been solved in the negative and the proof verified in Lean.\n     8→  </span></span>\n     9→    </div>\n    10→    <div id=\"content\">\n    11→      Is it true that there are no solutions to\\[n! = x^k\\pm y^k\\]with $x,y,n\\in \\mathbb{N}$, with $xy&gt;1$ and $k&gt;2$?\n    12→    </div>\n    13→    <div id=\"problem_id\">\n    14→      <a href=\"/399\">\n    15→        #399</a>: <a href=\"#cite-ErGr80-399\" onclick=\"event.preventDefault();addNewBox('ErGr80','399'); return false;\">[ErGr80,p.77]</a>\n    16→    </div>\n    17→    <div id=\"tags\">\n    18→      <a href=\"/tags/number theory\">\n    19→        number theory</a> | \n    20→\n    21→  <a href=\"/tags/factorials\">factorials</a>\n    22→    </div>\n    23→  </div>\n    24→  <div class=\"problem-additional-text\">\n    25→     \n    26→                    \n    27→                           Erdős and Obláth <a href=\"#cite-ErOb37-399\" onclick=\"event.preventDefault();addNewBox('ErOb37','399'); return false;\">[ErOb37]</a> proved this is true when $(x,y)=1$ and $k\\neq 4$. Pollack and Shapiro <a href=\"#cite-PoSh73-399\" onclick=\"event.preventDefault();addNewBox('PoSh73','399'); return false;\">[PoSh73]</a> proved there are no solutions to $n!=x^4-1$. The known methods break down without the condition $(x,y)=1$. \n    28→    <br>\n    29→    \n    30→    <br>\n    31→    \n    32→    Jonas Barfield has found the solution\\[10! = 48^4 - 36^4=12^4\\cdot 175.\\]Erdős and Obláth observed that the Bertrand-style fact (first proved by Breusch <a href=\"#cite-Br32-399\" onclick=\"event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Br32','399'); return false;\">[Br32]</a>) that, if $q_i$ is the sequence of primes congruent to $3\\pmod{4}$ then $q_{i+1}&lt;2q_i$ except for $q_1=3$, together with Fermat's theorem on the sums of two squares implies that the only solution to $n!=x^2+y^2$ is\\[6!=12^2+24^2.\\]Cambie has also observed that considerations modulo $8$ rule out any solutions to $n!=x^4+y^4$ with $(x,y)=1$ and $xy&gt;1$.\n    33→    <br>\n    34→    \n    35→    <br>\n    36→    \n    37→    This is discussed in problem D2 of Guy's collection <a href=\"#cite-Gu04-399\" onclick=\"event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Gu04','399'); return false;\">[Gu04]</a>.\n    38→                           \n    39→                    \n    40→                    \n    41→\n    42→    <p style=\"text-align: center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 90%;\"><a href=\"/latex/399\">View the LaTeX source</a>\n    43→    </p>\n    44→    <p style=\"text-align: center; font-family: 'Courier New', monospace; font-size: 90%;\">\n    45→      \n    46→  \n    47→ This page was last edited 30 September 2025. <a href=\"/history/399\">View history</a>\n    48→    </p>\n    49→    <div class=\"image-container\" id=\"image-container399\">\n    50→    </div>\n    51→  </div>\n    52→  <div class=\"problem-additional-text\" style=\"text-align:center;\">\n    53→  </div>\n    54→  <div id=\"bib-container399\">\n    55→  </div>\n    56→  <div class=\"external\">\n    57→    External data from <a href=\"https://github.com/teorth/erdosproblems\">the database</a> - you can help update this                \n    58→\n    59→\n    60→  \n    61→    <br>\n    62→    \n    63→    \n    64→  Formalised statement? \n    65→    \n    66→      <a href=\"https://github.com/google-deepmind/formal-conjectures/blob/