{"parentUuid":"68da5ec2-250c-408b-9a11-12f6326a9a20","isSidechain":false,"userType":"external","cwd":"/home/ryan/erdos","sessionId":"0888e9cf-982e-4295-bf22-57ee9e34505b","version":"2.1.69","gitBranch":"master","slug":"dapper-whistling-anchor","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019nrYJNzi4c4MmJcTf85zC6","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→import Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Central\n     2→import Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\n     3→import Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\n     4→import Mathlib.Order.Filter.Basic\n     5→import Mathlib.Order.Filter.Cofinite\n     6→\n     7→open BigOperators Finset\n     8→\n     9→/--\n    10→Erdős Problem #397 [ErGr80, p.77]:\n    11→\n    12→Are there only finitely many solutions to\n    13→  ∏ᵢ C(2mᵢ, mᵢ) = ∏ⱼ C(2nⱼ, nⱼ)\n    14→with the mᵢ, nⱼ distinct?\n    15→\n    16→DISPROVED by Somani (using ChatGPT). For any a ≥ 2, if c = 8a² + 8a + 1,\n    17→  C(2a,a) · C(4a+4,2a+2) · C(2c,c) = C(2a+2,a+1) · C(4a,2a) · C(2c+2,c+1).\n    18→This gives infinitely many solutions.\n    19→\n    20→The formalization states the negation: there are infinitely many pairs of\n    21→disjoint finite sets (M, N) of naturals such that the products of central\n    22→binomial coefficients over M and N are equal.\n    23→-/\n    24→theorem erdos_problem_397 :\n    25→    Set.Infinite\n    26→      {p : Finset ℕ × Finset ℕ |\n    27→        Disjoint p.1 p.2 ∧\n    28→        p.1.Nonempty ∧\n    29→        p.2.Nonempty ∧\n    30→        ∏ m ∈ p.1, Nat.centralBinom m = ∏ n ∈ p.2, Nat.centralBinom n} :=\n    31→  sorry\n    32→"}]},"uuid":"dd5ea6b5-8ecd-4a82-b0e6-5fd3b95328b9","timestamp":"2026-03-05T22:28:28.077Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ryan/erdos/conjectures/397.lean","content":"import Mathlib.Data.Nat.Choose.Central\nimport Mathlib.Data.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Order.Filter.Basic\nimport Mathlib.Order.Filter.Cofinite\n\nopen BigOperators Finset\n\n/--\nErdős Problem #397 [ErGr80, p.77]:\n\nAre there only finitely many solutions to\n  ∏ᵢ C(2mᵢ, mᵢ) = ∏ⱼ C(2nⱼ, nⱼ)\nwith the mᵢ, nⱼ distinct?\n\nDISPROVED by Somani (using ChatGPT). For any a ≥ 2, if c = 8a² + 8a + 1,\n  C(2a,a) · C(4a+4,2a+2) · C(2c,c) = C(2a+2,a+1) · C(4a,2a) · C(2c+2,c+1).\nThis gives infinitely many solutions.\n\nThe formalization states the negation: there are infinitely many pairs of\ndisjoint finite sets (M, N) of naturals such that the products of central\nbinomial coefficients over M and N are equal.\n-/\ntheorem erdos_problem_397 :\n    Set.Infinite\n      {p : Finset ℕ × Finset ℕ |\n        Disjoint p.1 p.2 ∧\n        p.1.Nonempty ∧\n        p.2.Nonempty ∧\n        ∏ m ∈ p.1, Nat.centralBinom m = ∏ n ∈ p.2, Nat.centralBinom n} :=\n  sorry\n","numLines":32,"startLine":1,"totalLines":32}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"68da5ec2-250c-408b-9a11-12f6326a9a20"}
